			"description": "Channel-authenticated endpoints for external systems. These endpoints require Channel JWT tokens (not regular user JWT tokens).\n\n**Channel Authentication Behavior:**\n\n**Channel JWT Token Structure:**\n- `key_version` (required): Key version for token validation (e.g., \"v1\", \"v2\")\n- `account_id` (optional): Account ID - if not provided, system looks up account by domain\n- `user_id` (optional): User ID - if not provided, system extracts from request body JWT (POST only)\n\n**Account Context Resolution:**\n1. If `account_id` is in channel JWT → use it directly\n2. If `account_id` is NOT in channel JWT → extract domain from Host header → lookup account in database\n\n**User Context Resolution:**\n1. If `user_id` is in channel JWT → use it directly\n2. If `user_id` is NOT in channel JWT → extract `jwtToken` from request body → parse user JWT → extract `userId`\n\n**Generate Channel Tokens:**\n```bash\n# With account_id and user_id:\npython3 create_channel_jwt.py 123 456 v1\n\n# With account_id only (user_id from body):\npython3 create_channel_jwt.py 123 None v1\n\n# Without account_id (domain lookup):\npython3 create_channel_jwt.py None None v1\n```\n\n**Usage Examples:**\n- Token with account_id: Works immediately, no Host header needed\n- Token without account_id: Must include `Host: <domain>` header for domain lookup\n- Token without user_id: For POST requests, user_id extracted from body JWT token"
